{0 A first CLI}

This tutorial writes [check], a command that runs test suites and shows
them as progress rows while they run, before printing a table of their
results. By the end, [check] has:

{ul
{- [--color], [--theme] and [--progress] options;}
{- plain lines on a pipe, or with [--progress=plain];}
{- a cram test whose output is the same on every machine.}}

Each section adds to the one before it.

{1:setup Setup}

[check] uses the [console] and [console-eio] packages, with [eio_main] to
run {{:https://github.com/ocaml-multicore/eio}Eio}, {{:https://erratique.ch/software/cmdliner}Cmdliner} for its options
and {{:https://erratique.ch/software/logs}Logs} for its messages:

{v
opam install console console-eio eio_main cmdliner logs
v}

Its [dune] file lists the libraries it uses:

{v
(executable
 (public_name check)
 (libraries
  console console.tty console-eio
  eio eio_main fmt fmt.cli cmdliner logs))
v}

{1:options Colour and theme}

A program decides on colour once, before it prints. {!Console_tty.setup}
reads {{:https://no-color.org/}[NO_COLOR]}, [TERM], [COLORTERM]
and the locale, and from them sets colour, colour depth and UTF-8 on the
standard outputs. Given a style renderer, [setup] uses that one instead, so
a [--color=auto|always|never] option read by [Fmt_cli.style_renderer] has
the last word.

A formatter also keeps a {{!Console.Theme}theme}. The table prints on
standard output and the progress rows on standard error, so [setup] gives
both outputs the theme that a [--theme] option reads:

{@ocaml[
let setup color theme =
  Console_tty.setup ?style_renderer:color ~theme ();
  Logs.set_level (Some Logs.Info)

let theme =
  Cmdliner.Arg.(
    value
    & opt (enum Console.Theme.all) Console.Theme.default
    & info [ "theme" ] ~doc:"The look of the output.")

let options = Cmdliner.Term.(const setup $ Fmt_cli.style_renderer () $ theme)
]}

{!Console.Theme.all} lists the predefined themes under their names, which
are the values [--theme] accepts:

{@ocaml[
# List.map fst Console.Theme.all;;
- : string list =
["default"; "ascii"; "dos"; "matrix"; "rainbow"; "dracula"; "nord";
 "solarized-dark"; "solarized-light"; "gruvbox-dark"; "gruvbox-light";
 "catppuccin-latte"; "catppuccin-mocha"; "tokyo-night"; "one-dark";
 "monokai"; "github-dark"; "github-light"]
]}

{!Console.Theme.default} takes the terminal's own colours, while a theme named
after a colour scheme, such as [dracula], is meant for a terminal that uses
that scheme.

{1:table A table}

A table is a list of columns and rows of {{!Console.View}views}. It is as
wide as its content needs, and no wider than the formatter's margin. Its
frame, header style and colours come from the theme of the formatter that
prints it:

{@ocaml[
# let suites = [ ("parser", 3); ("layout", 2) ];;
val suites : (string * int) list = [("parser", 3); ("layout", 2)]
# let results suites =
    let row (name, n) =
      [ Console.View.text name; Console.View.text (Int.to_string n) ]
    in
    Console.Table.(
      view
        [
          column (Console.View.text "Suite");
          column ~align:`Right (Console.View.text "Tests");
        ]
        (List.map row suites));;
val results : (string * int) list -> Console.View.t = <fun>
# Fmt.pr "%a@." Console.View.pp (results suites);;
╭────────┬───────╮
│ Suite  │ Tests │
├────────┼───────┤
│ parser │     3 │
│ layout │     2 │
╰────────┴───────╯
- : unit = ()
]}

{1:progress Progress rows}

{!Console_eio.run} shows progress rows on standard error, passes the
root of the work to a function, and ends the work when that function
returns. Inside, the function uses {!Console.Task.run} to run each piece
of work as a task under the root, whose row shows while it runs. The task
succeeds when its function returns and fails when it raises, and
{!Console.Task.set_items} says how much of its work is done:

{@ocaml[
let test root (name, total) =
  Console.Task.run root name @@ fun task ->
  for count = 1 to total do
    Console.Task.set_items task ~count ~total
  done;
  Logs.info (fun m -> m "%d tests passed" total)

let run_all ~clock ~progress suites =
  Console_eio.run ~clock ~progress ~label:"Testing" (fun root ->
      Eio.Fiber.List.iter (test root) suites)
]}

[Eio.Fiber.List.iter] runs each suite in a fiber of its own, so suites
that wait, for a child process for example, run side by side with one row
each. A row reads from the left: the suite's name, then its bar, up to 40
cells wide, then [count/total] and the time. The bars of all the rows start
in one column, one cell after the longest name, so each bar sits next to
its name and the counts line up. Below the rows, the summary line names the work given as [~label],
with its time and the count of suites that ended. It stays the last line
while the rows above it come and go, and it is the last line printed when
the work ends, before the message a program may end the root with
({!Console.Task.succeed}).

A suite that ends goes above the rows, into the terminal's scrollback,
where it stays: a task with tasks under it goes when it ends, with all of
them, each under its parent, so the scrollback reads as a tree.

A task's output shows under its row while it runs, and stays there if the
task fails. That output is made of:

{ul
{- the [Logs] messages logged inside it, as here;}
{- the lines it prints with [Fmt.pr];}
{- the lines it hands to {!Console.Task.log}.}}

Output printed outside any task goes above the rows instead, as
{{!Console_eio.output}where text goes} explains in more detail.

The rows and the summary are the default view, {!Console.Progress.view}.
A program can put its own view together from views: the table of results
above the rows, for instance, filled in as the suites run, which stays once
they are done. A {!Console.View.fit} part is laid out again at every frame,
so it shows the results so far:

{@ocaml[
let with_table ~table root =
  Console.View.vcat
    [
      Console.View.fit (fun _ -> Console.View.sized ~width:100 (table ()));
      Console.Progress.view root;
    ]
]}

[Console_eio.run ~view:(with_table ~table)] draws it, with [table] a
function that gives the table of the results so far. The display draws
the last lines of the view that fit on the terminal, so the table gives way
before the rows.

{1:program The whole program}

[check] sets up the outputs, runs the suites and then prints the table. It
also takes a [--progress] option, which says how progress is shown, as the
option of the same name does for
{{:https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/buildx/build/}[docker build]}:

{ul
{- [auto], the default, redraws the rows on a terminal;}
{- [tty] redraws them on any output;}
{- [plain] prints lines in turn, as on a pipe, even on a terminal;}
{- [quiet] prints only what fails;}
{- [json] writes one JSON object for each event
   ({{!Console.Task.json}JSON events}).}}

{!Console.Progress.all} lists these values in the form Cmdliner reads, for
the [progress] argument of {!Console_eio.run}:

{@ocaml[
let progress =
  Cmdliner.Arg.(
    value
    & opt (enum Console.Progress.all) `Auto
    & info [ "progress" ]
        ~doc:
          "How progress shows: $(b,auto), $(b,tty), $(b,plain), $(b,quiet) or \
           $(b,json).")

let check () progress =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  run_all ~clock:(Eio.Stdenv.mono_clock env) ~progress suites;
  Fmt.pr "%a@." Console.View.pp (results suites)

let cmd =
  Cmdliner.Cmd.v (Cmdliner.Cmd.info "check")
    Cmdliner.Term.(const check $ options $ progress)

let main () = exit (Cmdliner.Cmd.eval cmd)
]}

The program's entry point is then [let () = main ()].

{1:pipe Output on a pipe}

When the output is not a terminal, as in a pipe or a CI log, nothing is
redrawn, and [check] prints lines in turn instead:

{ul
{- a task prints a line when it starts;}
{- each of its messages follows, after its path, the labels from its top
   task down to it;}
{- it prints a line when it ends, with its count and its time, then
   [failed] or [cancelled] if it did not succeed;}
{- the summary comes last, then the message the work ended with, if any.}}

Unless [--color=always] asks for it, a pipe gets no colour. Here [check]
runs with its output captured and with [CONSOLE_DETERMINISTIC_CLOCK] set,
which the {{!testing}next section} explains:

{@ocaml[
# Unix.putenv "CONSOLE_DETERMINISTIC_CLOCK" "1";;
- : unit = ()
# Cmdliner.Cmd.eval ~argv:[| "check"; "--theme=dos" |] cmd;;
[-] parser
  parser: 3 tests passed
[+] parser  3/3  0.0s
[-] layout
  layout: 2 tests passed
[+] layout  2/2  0.0s
[+] Testing 0.0s (2/2)
╔════════╤═══════╗
║ Suite  │ Tests ║
╟────────┼───────╢
║ parser │     3 ║
║ layout │     2 ║
╚════════╧═══════╝
- : int = 0
]}

The marks at the start of each line come from the theme, here
{!Console.Theme.dos}. Console flushes standard output before each line it
writes, so [check 2>&1 | cat] keeps the two outputs in the order they were
written.

[check --progress=plain] prints the same lines on a terminal. With
[--progress=quiet] it prints none of them, because no suite fails, and
only the table remains:

{@ocaml[
# Cmdliner.Cmd.eval ~argv:[| "check"; "--progress=quiet" |] cmd;;
╭────────┬───────╮
│ Suite  │ Tests │
├────────┼───────┤
│ parser │     3 │
│ layout │     2 │
╰────────┴───────╯
- : int = 0
]}

With [--progress=json], [check] writes the events for another program to
read instead, one JSON object to a line ({{!Console.Task.json}JSON
events}). An OCaml program reads each line back as a
{!Console.Task.Event.t} with {!Console.Task.Event.of_string}, which
decodes it with the jsont codec {!Console.Task.Event.jsont}, and reads
its members with accessors such as {!Console.Task.Event.val-kind} and
{!Console.Task.Event.text}.

{1:testing Testing the output}

The elapsed times are the only part of the output that changes from one
machine to another. When [CONSOLE_DETERMINISTIC_CLOCK] is set to a value
other than the empty string, {!Console_eio.run} reads a clock stopped at
zero, unless the program gives it one of {!Console_eio.clock} (below):

{ul
{- every time reads [0.0s];}
{- no rate shows;}
{- the spinners and the motion stand still.}}

A {{:https://dune.build/}Dune} cram test of [check] then reads
the same on every machine. Dune runs it without a UTF-8 locale, so [check]
draws its marks and its table in ASCII. It needs a [dune] stanza that
makes [check] available:

{v
(cram
 (deps %{bin:check}))
v}

and a file, [check.t], with the session it expects:

{v
  $ export CONSOLE_DETERMINISTIC_CLOCK=1
  $ check 2>&1
  [-] parser
    parser: 3 tests passed
  [+] parser  3/3  0.0s
  [-] layout
    layout: 2 tests passed
  [+] layout  2/2  0.0s
  [+] Testing 0.0s (2/2)
  +--------+-------+
  | Suite  | Tests |
  +--------+-------+
  | parser |     3 |
  | layout |     2 |
  +--------+-------+
v}

A program that sleeps on the clock {!Console_eio.clock} gives it, and
passes that clock to {!Console_eio.run}, keeps its times: the clock's time
is virtual, a sleep waking at the time it started plus its duration,
whatever the program computes between them, so the times read the same on
every machine
({{!Console_eio.section-deterministic}a deterministic clock}).

The widgets need no clock at all. [Console.View.to_string] and the other
[to_string] functions return the text that a unit test compares, without
escape codes unless [~styled:true] asks for them.

